% ============================================================
% 5. DIF AS DIAGNOSTIC LENS
% ============================================================
\section{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\subsection{Benchmark Redundancy and Discriminative Signal}
\label{sec:redundancy}

Removing all C-category DIF items (31\% of MMLU) barely changes model rankings: the Spearman correlation between full and stable-only rankings is $\rho \approx 0.997$. This reveals massive measurement redundancy---MMLU has approximately $3\times$ more items than needed for stable rankings.

Yet DIF items are not noise. Their removal disrupts rankings \emph{significantly more} than random removal of equal size ($z = -8$ to $-49\sigma$ across 21 conditions). DIF items carry $1.09\times$ cross-cohort accuracy swing versus $0.96\times$ for stable items---they concentrate the information that differentiates model generations. Model-specific patterns emerge: phi-3 and llama-3 families gain $+700$ to $+850$ ranks from DIF items, while mistral merges lose $-400$ to $-587$ ranks.

DIF items thus function as \emph{diagnostic markers}: they identify which capability dimensions are changing across model generations. This reframes DIF from ``find bad items and remove'' to ``understand what changed and why''---complementing Growing Pains' anchor-item approach by detecting which anchors are degrading.


\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K and GSM1K}
\label{sec:cross_benchmark}

On GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\times$ 6{,}702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$; partial $r = -0.385$ controlling for accuracy; permutation $p = 0.014$). Models with high contamination gaps (Phi, Gemma families) perform \emph{worse} on DIF items. This confirms that DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items that contamination cannot fake---contaminated models achieve shallow performance gains concentrated on non-DIF items. The analysis includes $N = 33$ matched models and is leave-one-out robust (min $|r| = 0.374$).


\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF for Operational Monitoring}
\label{sec:bayesian}

Single-shot DIF detection has limited power for medium effects ($\text{TPR} = 0.42$ at $\delta = 1.0$). We propose cumulative Bayesian DIF priors that accumulate evidence across successive benchmark administrations.

Table~\ref{tab:bayesian} shows power improvement over four simulated administrations ($N = 200$ models per administration). Each administration adds approximately 15 percentage points of TPR for medium effects ($\delta = 1.0$), reaching 0.71 after three administrations. FPR remains stable at $\sim$0.04 because Bayesian updating with proper priors is conservative on non-DIF items. At the operational threshold ($\delta = 1.4$), power reaches 0.89 by administration 4. The Bayes factor threshold of 3.2 (``substantial evidence'') provides a principled decision criterion superior to the arbitrary $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ cutoff.

