% ============================================================
% 8. CONCLUSION
% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We present the largest-scale psychometric analysis of an LLM benchmark, revealing that 38\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations. This instability persists within every subject domain, survives architecture stratification (22.1\% within decoder-only models), and correlates with capability improvement---models getting better at specific domains changes how items function.

Our analysis exposes a fundamental blind spot in the field's validation methodology: web-overlap contamination labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. The semi-synthetic validation framework we introduce---with cross-subject matching to eliminate FPR inflation---provides reusable infrastructure for this purpose.

Perhaps most importantly, DIF items are not measurement noise to be discarded. They concentrate the discriminative signal that differentiates model generations, and they anti-correlate with contamination. DIF is a diagnostic lens that reveals \emph{which} capability dimensions are changing---complementing, rather than competing with, existing benchmark integrity efforts.


