% ============================================================
% ABSTRACT
% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score of 80\% today measures the same construct as 80\% a year ago. We challenge this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, a classical psychometric tool, to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. We report four findings. \textbf{First}, 38\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations (ETS C category, $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$), persisting within every subject domain (per-subject C\% = 31--45\%). \textbf{Second}, the field's standard validation approach---web-overlap contamination labels---cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment $\approx 1.0$), while semi-synthetic injection confirms detection capability ($\Delta\text{TPR} = +0.508$ on MMLU, $+0.818$ on GSM8K). \textbf{Third}, we provide a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR} = 0.000$). \textbf{Fourth}, DIF items concentrate discriminative signal rather than noise: their removal disrupts rankings more than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\sigma$), and DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination on GSM8K ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$). Architecture stratification reduces spurious flags by 29\% (C\% from 31.3\% to 22.1\%) while confirming that substantial genuine instability persists, demonstrating the method is refinable rather than fundamentally confounded. We release the semi-synthetic framework and DIF diagnostics as open infrastructure for benchmark maintenance.
\end{abstract}

% ============================================================
% 1. INTRODUCTION
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

When we state that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct---that the 12{,}508 items function identically regardless of when or by which model they are answered. This assumption of \emph{temporal score comparability} underpins every leaderboard ranking, every benchmark-based paper claim, and every policy decision that cites benchmark performance.

But do MMLU items function the same across model generations? As models improve through successive training paradigms---scaling, instruction tuning, RLHF, reasoning-chain RL---the population of test-takers changes dramatically. In classical psychometrics, when a test item functions differently across groups (e.g., producing different correct-response probabilities at matched ability levels), the item exhibits \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF). DIF does not mean an item is ``bad''---it means the item's measurement properties are group-dependent, threatening the comparability of scores derived from that item.

We apply DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix \citep{metabench2024}, comprising 5{,}227 models evaluated on 12{,}508 MMLU items. This constitutes the largest-scale psychometric analysis of an LLM benchmark to date. Our analysis yields four contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Temporal instability characterization.} 38\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This persists within every subject domain (31--45\% per subject, mean 38.1\%) and survives architecture stratification (22.1\% within decoder-only models alone). The instability correlates with domain-specific accuracy improvement ($\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), suggesting it reflects genuine capability shifts rather than pure noise.

    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels \citep{li2024contamination} measure global exposure---whether an item is publicly accessible---not differential contamination. DIF enrichment $\approx 1.0$ against these labels is expected, not failure. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detection capability ($\Delta\text{TPR} = +0.508$). This blind spot affects all work validating item-level detectors against web-overlap ground truth.

    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We introduce a $\Delta$TPR methodology that injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices. Cross-subject matching (using total score on \emph{other} subjects as the Mantel-Haenszel matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR} = 0.000$ across 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (+0.186 FPR inflation). The framework generalizes to GSM8K ($\Delta\text{TPR} = +0.818$).

    \item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} MMLU rankings exhibit massive redundancy ($\rho \approx 0.997$ after 31\% item removal), yet DIF items concentrate discriminative signal: their removal disrupts rankings significantly more than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\sigma$). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K confirms DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$)---contaminated models perform \emph{worse} on DIF items, which capture genuine capability differences.
\end{enumerate}

Architecture-stratified analysis (Section~\ref{sec:arch_strat}) demonstrates that restricting DIF to decoder-only models reduces C\% from 31.3\% to 22.1\%---a 29\% reduction---while improving IRT model fit (RMSEA from 0.082 to 0.037). This transforms the ``architecture confounding'' objection into a practical recommendation: run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations.


% ============================================================
% 2. RELATED WORK
% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}
Contamination detection methods broadly fall into two categories. \emph{Per-model} approaches analyze individual model outputs: Min-K\% \citep{shi2024mink} uses minimum $k$-th percentile log-likelihood as a memorization signal; ConStat \citep{constat2024} compares performance on original versus semantically reformulated items; DVD \citep{dvd2024} measures generation variance under temperature sampling to detect bimodal memorization signatures. These methods share a reliance on web-overlap or n-gram labels for validation---a gap we characterize in Section~\ref{sec:meta_critique}. Preliminary evaluation of ConStat on MMLU shows AUROC of 0.73 on non-RL models but degradation to 0.44 on RL models, with low correlation to DIF flags ($r = 0.21$), confirming orthogonal detection axes. DVD achieves moderate AUROC (0.66 for verbatim contamination) at high computational cost (65 GPU-hours for 12 models), with similarly low DIF correlation ($r = 0.15$).

\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}
Differential Item Functioning analysis has a 40-year history in psychometrics \citep{holland1993dif}. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \citep{mantel1959mh} remains the most widely used method due to its simplicity and robust statistical properties. The ETS classification system (A/B/C categories) provides operational thresholds: C items ($|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ and significantly different from 1.0) warrant review or removal. Item-Focused Trees \citep{tutz2016ift} extend DIF detection to continuous covariates, enabling finer temporal resolution. We include an IFT analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ift} showing concordance with categorical DIF (Jaccard = 0.76) and temporal split points aligning with major model release waves.

\paragraph{Benchmark integrity and maintenance.}
Growing Pains \citep{growingpains2024} identifies anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic complements this by detecting which anchor items are degrading across model generations. LiveBench \citep{livebench2024} and similar dynamic benchmarks address contamination through item refresh; our analysis provides psychometric infrastructure for deciding \emph{which} items to refresh and validating that replacements maintain construct equivalence.


% ============================================================
% 3. METHOD
% ============================================================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Differential Item Functioning: A Primer for ML Researchers}
\label{sec:dif_primer}

Consider a binary response matrix $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^{N \times J}$ where $X_{ij} = 1$ if model $i$ answers item $j$ correctly. In DIF analysis, we partition models into a \emph{reference group} $R$ and a \emph{focal group} $F$ (here: temporal cohorts, e.g., pre-2024 vs.\ 2024-H1 models) and ask: does item $j$ function differently across groups, \emph{after conditioning on overall ability}?

The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure conditions on a discrete matching variable (total score or score stratum $k$) and computes the common odds ratio:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH}} = \frac{\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}
\end{equation}
where $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2 \times 2$ table (group $\times$ response) at stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds ratio is converted to the ETS $\Delta$ scale:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{MH}} = -2.35 \ln(\hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH}})
\end{equation}

Items are classified using the ETS system:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{A} (negligible): $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ or not statistically significant
    \item \textbf{B} (moderate): $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and significant
    \item \textbf{C} (large): $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ and significantly different from 1.0
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{ML translation.} DIF analysis is analogous to checking whether a feature's predictive relationship with the outcome changes across subpopulations after controlling for a confounder. An item with large DIF is one where the ``decision boundary'' between correct and incorrect shifts across model generations at matched ability---the item measures something different for newer models than for older ones.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}
\label{sec:semi_synthetic}

Validating item-level contamination detectors requires ground truth at the item$\times$model level---which items are contaminated \emph{for which models}. No such ground truth exists naturally. We construct it via \emph{semi-synthetic injection}: starting from the real METABENCH response matrix, we flip a fraction $p$ of incorrect responses to correct for selected items in the focal group, simulating differential contamination.

The key metric is $\Delta$TPR: the increase in DIF detection rate from injection. If a detector responds to injected differential contamination, $\Delta\text{TPR} > 0$; if it cannot distinguish injected items from naturally unstable ones, $\Delta\text{TPR} \approx 0$.

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} A critical methodological choice is the MH matching variable. When injecting contamination into subject $s$, using the total score on subject $s$ as the matching variable inflates FPR because the matching variable itself is contaminated. We instead match on the total score on \emph{all other subjects} $S \setminus \{s\}$. This eliminates FPR inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR} = 0.000$ across all 5 tested subjects) while preserving detection power ($\Delta\text{TPR} > 0.3$ in 4/5 subjects).


% ============================================================
% 4. EXPERIMENTS: TEMPORAL DIF ON MMLU
% ============================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Large-Scale Temporal DIF Analysis}
\label{sec:temporal_dif}

\paragraph{Setup.} We analyze the METABENCH response matrix: 5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items. Models are partitioned into temporal cohorts based on release date (reference: pre-2024; focal: 2024-H1). The MH procedure is applied to each item with total MMLU score as the matching variable.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:main_dif} summarizes the main findings. 31.3\% of items fall into ETS category C (large DIF) in the full-population analysis. A random-split sanity check (randomly assigning models to groups) yields C\% = 0.18\%, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact. Within-subject analysis (running DIF separately per MMLU subject) reveals C\% ranges from 31\% to 45\% across subjects, with mean 38.1\%---instability is pervasive, not concentrated in a few subjects.

The correlation between subject-level C\% and subject-level accuracy improvement is $\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$), indicating that domains where models improved most also exhibit greater item instability. This is consistent with the interpretation that rapid capability improvement changes how items function---models that ``got better'' at a domain interact differently with items testing that domain.

DIF enrichment against Li et al.\ web-overlap contamination labels is 0.96 ($\approx 1.0$), a result we analyze in Section~\ref{sec:meta_critique}.

