\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:meta_critique}

The near-unity enrichment ($\approx 1.0$) between DIF flags and web-overlap labels is expected rather than surprising. Web-overlap labels \citep{li2024contamination} measure \emph{global exposure}---whether an item text appears on the public internet. Since virtually all MMLU items have been on Common Crawl since 2020, nearly all items have equal global exposure. DIF, by contrast, detects \emph{differential functioning}---whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts at matched ability. These are orthogonal signals: global exposure is a static, model-agnostic property; differential functioning is dynamic and group-specific.

This has a broader implication: \emph{any} contamination detection method validated against web-overlap labels at the item level faces the same blind spot. If the ground truth cannot distinguish ``contaminated for model A but not model B'' from ``equally exposed to all models,'' it cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This affects Min-K\%, n-gram methods, and all work using web-overlap as ground truth for item-level validation.

Semi-synthetic injection (Section~\ref{sec:semi_synthetic_results}) confirms that DIF \emph{can} detect differential contamination when it exists ($\Delta\text{TPR} = +0.508$)---the problem is the ground truth, not the detector.


\subsection{Architecture-Stratified DIF Analysis}
\label{sec:arch_strat}

A natural concern is that DIF flags may reflect architecture differences (encoder-decoder vs.\ decoder-only vs.\ mixture-of-experts) rather than genuine temporal instability. We address this directly by restricting DIF analysis to decoder-only models only ($N = 3{,}847$; reference: $n = 892$, focal: $n = 541$).

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:arch_irt} shows IRT model fit diagnostics. Restricting to decoder-only models dramatically improves fit: RMSEA drops from 0.082 (poor) to 0.037 (acceptable), CFI increases to 0.93, and SRMR falls to 0.028. This confirms that architecture heterogeneity was driving model misspecification, not the items themselves.

Table~\ref{tab:arch_dif} presents DIF detection rates. C\% drops from 31.3\% to 22.1\%---a 29.4\% reduction---meaning approximately 9 percentage points of the original flags were architecture-confounded. The remaining 22.1\% represents genuine temporal instability within a homogeneous architecture population. Semi-synthetic validation improves correspondingly: FPR drops from 0.314 to 0.221, and $\Delta$TPR increases from 0.674 to 0.72.

A sanity check (random split within decoder-only models) yields C\% = 0.12\%, confirming the reduction is not from power loss. Flag overlap with the full-population analysis (Jaccard = 0.68) shows the core DIF items persist---architecture stratification refines rather than contradicts the original finding.

