\subsection{Structural Constraints on DIF}
\label{sec:structural}

We investigated whether structural improvements to DIF methodology could reduce the baseline instability rate. Three approaches were tested:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Matched-ability cohort design}: Balancing temporal cohorts on overall ability does not reduce C\% below 31\%, because DIF is already ability-conditioned.
    \item \textbf{IRT $\theta$-conditioning}: Replacing discrete score strata with continuous IRT ability estimates yields near-identical flags ($r = 0.9986$ with MH flags), confirming discretization is not the issue.
    \item \textbf{Bifactor pre-check}: A bifactor IRT model (general + subject-specific factors) confirms MMLU has genuine multidimensional structure (general factor: 42\% variance, subject factors: 18\% average; RMSEA = 0.038, CFI = 0.91). However, anchoring on DIF-free items reduces C\% only to 33.5\%---multidimensionality accounts for merely $\sim$2.4 pp of the instability.
\end{enumerate}

Table~\ref{tab:bifactor} summarizes the bifactor analysis. The equated score correlation ($\rho = 0.965$) meets the $\geq 0.95$ criterion, but this reflects MMLU's massive redundancy rather than successful decontamination. These results constitute an empirical observation: standard psychometric corrections---matched-ability, $\theta$-conditioning, bifactor equating---are insufficient to reduce baseline DIF below 31\% while maintaining detection power. Higher-dimensional methods (MIMIC, multidimensional IRT, propensity-score matching) may address individual constraints and are directions for future work.

