% ============================================================
% 6. DISCUSSION
% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{RL robustness.}
A natural concern is whether reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO-trained) break DIF analysis. We evaluate this in Appendix~\ref{app:rl}: adding 13 reasoning-RL models to METABENCH cohorts barely perturbs DIF flags (Jaccard = 0.88, TOST equivalence $p = 0.008$). By contrast, traditional per-model detectors degrade sharply on RL models (Min-K\% AUROC: 0.81 $\rightarrow$ 0.52; ConStat AUROC: 0.77 $\rightarrow$ 0.48). DIF's group-level architecture is fundamentally orthogonal to the NLL-gap-contraction mechanism that defeats per-model detectors. The $N = 13$ sample is small and we acknowledge this as a limitation; the directional evidence is nonetheless unambiguous.

\paragraph{Cause vs.\ characterization.}
We emphasize that 38\% instability is a \emph{characterization finding}, not a contamination claim. The correlation with accuracy improvement ($\rho = 0.307$) means we cannot decompose instability into contamination vs.\ genuine capability improvement. DIF reveals \emph{that} items function differently---not \emph{why}. This is the appropriate scope for a characterization study; causal decomposition requires controlled experiments (training models with and without specific items) that are beyond current infrastructure.


