\paragraph{Automated benchmark maintenance.}
The massive redundancy we observe ($\rho = 0.997$ after 31\% item removal) suggests benchmark maintenance via item replacement is feasible. Preliminary analysis (Table~\ref{tab:degradation}) confirms MMLU tolerates aggressive replacement: $\rho = 0.994$ at 20\% replacement with 96\% construct coverage. A pilot item generation study using student simulation \citep{studentsim2025} achieves difficulty calibration $\rho = 0.71$ (Table~\ref{tab:itemgen}), substantially outperforming prompt-based targeting ($\rho = 0.28$). These components---DIF identifies which items to replace, student simulation calibrates replacements, graceful degradation bounds validate the process---form a complete pipeline for future work.

