\section{Item-Focused Trees Analysis}
\label{app:ift}

Item-Focused Trees \citep{tutz2016ift} treat model release date as a continuous covariate (days since 2023-01-01) rather than imposing categorical cohort splits. Table~\ref{tab:ift} compares IFT with categorical DIF across 12{,}508 items.

IFT identifies 28.4\% of items as functionally unstable (vs.\ 31.3\% categorical)---slightly fewer because continuous modeling avoids artificial inflation from binary cohort splits. Jaccard overlap is 0.76, with 412 unique IFT detections capturing items with smooth temporal drift (34\% show gradual transition patterns). Semi-synthetic AUC is 0.91 (vs.\ 0.88 categorical). Tree split points cluster around 2023-Q4 and 2024-Q2, aligning with major model release waves (Llama-2$\rightarrow$3, Qwen-1.5$\rightarrow$2), providing temporal resolution that categorical DIF cannot offer.

